  <body>
	<pre id="output"></pre>
	<div id="storyData" style="display: none;">
	  <tw-storydata name="Frog Game Dialogue" startnode="1" creator="Twine" creator-version="2.11.1" format="Twison" format-version="0.0.1" ifid="D818D820-2029-46E8-B59E-D981DD3A9B14" options="" tags="" zoom="1" hidden><style role="stylesheet" id="twine-user-stylesheet" type="text/twine-css"></style><script role="script" id="twine-user-script" type="text/twine-javascript"></script><tw-passagedata pid="1" name="Goldfish-01" tags="" position="100,100" size="100,100">Ahhhhh!!!!! You&#39;re finally here! It&#39;s been too long! Too long I can&#39;t remeber how long it have been!

[[Goldfish-02]]


</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="2" name="Goldfish-02" tags="" position="225,100" size="100,100">Where've you been!? Don't you know what’s happened here? ...Why are you still here? Shouldn’t you be going to check the situation out right away?

[[Goldfish-03]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="3" name="Goldfish-03" tags="" position="350,100" size="100,100">AHHHHHHHHHH!!!

[[Goldfish-04]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="4" name="Goldfish-04" tags="" position="475,100" size="100,100">Ahhh! You&#39;re finally here...Wait, am I repeating myself? I can&#39;t remember! I think one of my scales has fallen off, my owner won’t be happy about this at all! No, wait… Owner? Where has my owner gone? Where even am I? Why is the water so terrifyingly deep here?

[[Goldfish-05]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="5" name="Goldfish-05" tags="" position="600,100" size="100,100">You&#39;re going to help me, right? Right?? Yeah, you should definetly do something.  Well, it&#39;s not like you really have a choice, this place is in big big trouble. The flood is going to kill EVERYONE! Now what are you still staring at me for? Go follow that path! Ahhhhhh!</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="6" name="Merchant-01" tags="" position="100,225" size="100,100">Welcome, welcome! Thank you for coming by during this particularly difficult time...Anyway, you have those adorable little seed beads with you, don't you?

[[Merchant-02]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="7" name="Merchant-02" tags="" position="225,225" size="100,100">Our people are collectors. Although the current water level and chaos has blocked me from gathering more stuff, I&#39;m sure you can still find something useful here. You can always trade beeds with others as well, I&#39;m sure my fellow traders will be hanging around all over the pond.

[[Merchant-farewell]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="8" name="Merchant-farewell" tags="" position="350,225" size="100,100">Come again!
</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="9" name="Merchant-repeat" tags="" position="475,225" size="100,100">Forget something? Take your time.</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="10" name="Goldfish-repeat" tags="" position="725,100" size="100,100">Bubbles that rise past my little glass castle
Flakes drifting down like a golden-food hassle
Pebbles that sparkle when sunlight comes streaming
These are a few of my favorite things...Ah! What are you, uh, doing here? Hey, make yourself useful and get out of here! No more scales for you!</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="11" name="Elder-01" tags="" position="100,425" size="100,100">Ah, so here you are, outsider. From the very day your unusual blue skin first appeared in Antlia, I knew you would change the fate of this place. Now that disaster has struck, what role will you play in the events to come? Hmm… interesting.

[[Elder-02]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="12" name="Elder-02" tags="" position="225,425" size="100,100">As you can see, the rest of the frog clan are all busy dealing with the disaster and rebuilding. I’m the only one left here. I’m old and nearly blind. What more can I do? What use am I to you?

[[Elder-withbutton]]

[[Elder-withoutbutton]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="13" name="Elder-withbutton" tags="" position="350,350" size="100,100">You are no child. But I see you’re carrying a button. How nostalgic… I used to collect buttons, just as I used to collect knowledge. But the children have all grown up, my eyesight has failed, and before I knew it, I’d lost them all. The children of today no longer enjoy listening to my stories either. Yet my memories remain intact. Would you like to make a swap? If you give me the buttons, I’ll tell you an ancient story—one as old as the buttons themselves.</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="14" name="Elder-withoutbutton" tags="" position="350,475" size="100,100">I used to collect buttons, just as I used to collect knowledge. You are no child, but if you find any button, bring it to me, I’ll tell you an ancient story—one as old as the buttons themselves. Outsider, are you interested in a deal like this?</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="15" name="Elder-Intro" tags="" position="475,425" size="100,100">It’s good to see you again, outsider. Since you’ve brought the button, get ready to hear a story.</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="16" name="Elder-story01a" tags="" position="600,350" size="100,100">The first tale is older even than the crack in this button. Custos Ordinis, the Guardian of the Order of Antlia, has existed since before the dawn of time. No living species knows why it exists, or how it maintains the water body at the centre of Antlia at the proper water level. The Custos Ordinis seeks neither speech nor offerings. We, all living creatures, must simply fulfil our respective duties within its silence, focusing on our own destinies, and pray that it may forever preserve the peace and tranquillity of our world.

[[Elder-story01b]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="17" name="Elder-story01b" tags="" position="725,350" size="100,100">No creature can, on its own, interfere with the workings of the Custos Ordinis. It is far too vast and too formidable. Over the ages, the sentient species of Antlia have come to understand that they must not meddle with its sacred will… yet the Order of Serpens is an exception. These reclusive serpents have long occupied the corner of the earth closest to the Custos Ordinis. They are the largest and most powerful species in the whole of Antlia. Despite their small numbers, they lack any sense of reverence for the Custos Ordinis. In my youth, a serpent once dared to coil upon the Custos Ordinis for the duration of a single sunrise and sunset. And during the village’s last mating season, a great serpent even attempted to disrupt the flow of the water with its long tail.

[[Elder-story01c]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="18" name="Elder-story01c" tags="" position="850,350" size="100,100">Has such behaviour angered the sacred Custos Ordinis? What exactly are their intentions? And to what extent can we, as sentient beings, truly understand and interfere with the existence of the Custos Ordinis?

[[Elder-closure]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="19" name="Elder-closure" tags="" position="1000,450" size="100,100">Outsider, you must think carefully about this story. If not through learning, how else can we come to understand the world—and, more importantly, ourselves?</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="20" name="Elder-story02a" tags="" position="600,475" size="100,100">The second tale is one about Antlia itself—not about who lives here, but about what this place was before any of us did. The elders before me said that Antlia was not always a settlement beside water. It was carved, they say, from a single drop that never finished falling.

[[Elder-story02b]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="21" name="Elder-story03a" tags="" position="600,600" size="100,100">There is a third tale, outsider, and it is the one I like least to tell. Not because it frightens me, but because I cannot finish it. No frog can. Just as the edge of this button has neither a beginning nor an end, so too does this story.

[[Elder-story03b]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="22" name="Nightheron-01" tags="" position="100,800" size="100,100">It stands motionless in the shallows, taller than any bird has a right to be, feathers slicked dark and heavy as though the pond itself had grown feathers. Two eyes, yellow and unblinking, fix on you before you have decided whether to approach.

A rattle rises from its throat. Then, slowly, as though dragging the words up from somewhere older than speech:

[[Nightheron-02]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="23" name="Elder-story02b" tags="" position="725,475" size="100,100">Picture it, outsider: a drop of water suspended between sky and ground, too heavy to rise, too light to fall. Time passed around it rather than through it. Inside that stillness, the first creatures took shape—not born, but settled, the way silt settles at the bottom of a pond. We are not descendants of anything. We are sediment. We are what remained once the drop stopped deciding.

[[Elder-story02c]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="24" name="Elder-story02c" tags="" position="850,475" size="100,100">However, is that the truth of this world? If this is the essence of our lives, and indeed the essence of Antlia as a whole, then what role does the Custos Ordinis play within it? Is it, too, shaped by the sediment? Or might that single drop of water also be a creation of its will? Is there a will even vaster and more unfathomable than that? And what role have we been assigned within it?

[[Elder-closure]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="25" name="Elder-story03b" tags="" position="725,600" size="100,100">When I was young, I once found a fragment near the ditch&#39;s northern rim—smooth, harder than any stone, with markings pressed into it too even to be the work of claw or beak. My mother told me not to touch it. She said such things wash in sometimes from beyond the mist, remnants of the ones who came before the Custos Ordinis and, some whisper, who placed it there themselves.

[[Elder-story03c]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="26" name="Elder-story03c" tags="" position="850,600" size="100,100">The ones who came before. We have no word for them that isn&#39;t borrowed or half-invented. The towering presence, taller than a thousand villages stacked upon one another. No creature, save for birds, has ever caught a glimpse of their full form. It is said that even birds keep their distance from them.

[[Elder-story03d]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="27" name="Elder-story03d" tags="" position="975,600" size="100,100">What troubles me, outsider, is not whether they exist. It is that everything which keeps us alive—the Custos Ordinis, the water level, the very order of Antlia—behaves as though it was built for a purpose, by something that understood purpose. A frog does not build a heart. A frog is simply given one. But the Custos Ordinis feels built. And if it was built, outsider... then someone built it. Someone who has not been seen since, and who may not know, or care, that we are living inside their leftover work.

[[Elder-closure]] </tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="28" name="Nightheron-02" tags="" position="225,800" size="100,100">...You. Not frog. Not fish. Something else.

[[Nightheron-03]] </tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="29" name="Nightheron-03" tags="" position="350,800" size="100,100">They send small things to me. Frightened things. You are not frightened. Should you be?

[[Nightheron-04]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="30" name="Nightheron-04" tags="" position="475,800" size="100,100">I carry things. Across the water. Around the mist. Far, if far is needed.

[[Nightheron-05]] </tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="31" name="Nightheron-05" tags="" position="600,800" size="100,100">Red creature. Dragonfly. Full of anger. Too dangerous.

[[Nightheron-06]] </tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="32" name="Nightheron-06" tags="" position="725,800" size="100,100">Contract. Need to fullfil. Not right now. Later.

[[Nightheron-repeat]]</tw-passagedata><tw-passagedata pid="33" name="Nightheron-repeat" tags="" position="850,800" size="100,100">Later.</tw-passagedata></tw-storydata>
	</div>
